WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.629
Today we're gonna have a look at data

00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:05.339
visualisation. Data visualisation is a

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:08.309
way of representing information in a

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:11.309
artistic way, in an interesting way;

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:13.349
scientists might be happy with scatter

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:16.230
plots and black and white boring graphs

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.490
but if you want to communicate

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:21.480
information and data to the public you

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.890
want it to be fun, you want it to be

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.689
enjoyable, you want it to
be colourful. And

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:27.539
we thought we might have a look at a

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:30.300
particular guy's work, a guy called Martin

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.040
Krzywinski. Today we're actually going to

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.670
look at some of his more numbery but

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.620
artistic work. So we're gonna have a look

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.670
at him finding beauty 
shall we say, artistry,

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.660
in the randomness of pi. So we've got it

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.539
down here, this is pi, in a beautiful

00:00:48.539 --> 00:00:50.820
colourful way. Now this is first of all

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.440
the most simple way I think that you

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.360
could have done this, all he's done is

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.460
taken the digits of pi and he's given

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.680
each digit a different colour. So it

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.510
starts with 3, so it looks like 3

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:04.080
is an orange colour there. 1 is a red,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.860
4 is a yellow, 1 again is red and

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.979
then 5 is green, and I think that's

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:13.140
a 9 that's purple - it's made this

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.009
really beautiful poster. You can kind of

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.600
see the randomness of it though, I don't

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.570
see there's any particular pattern to

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.180
the colours; which reflects the

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.220
randomness of the digits of pi as well.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.320
He took that a step further and he

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.340
started to colour the centre of the

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:34.650
circles using the colour of the next

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.650
digit. I think he only did that just to

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.350
make it a little bit prettier; but he

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.060
was- I think he found it interesting

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:46.350
because he started to join up digits

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.720
that had the same colours in them, which

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:49.829
looks like this, there you go.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:53.189
Can you see that- so he's connected

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:56.130
digits or adjacent digits in his poster

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.649
that have the same colour, so they'd be

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:00.360
the same number. And he kind of makes

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.899
these disconnected networks. I don't

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:05.820
think there's any great mathematical truth

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.970
to find underneath this; this is for art.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.489
And Martin's very clear about this

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:12.780
himself; this

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.050
is less about the mathematics and more

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.660
about the beauty of it. But it is

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.120
intriguing as well. The other way you

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.700
could do this is if you put the digits

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.930
of pi in a spiral like this. This kind of

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.810
reminds me of the tiling, you know, in a

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.550
Roman bathhouse, something like that,

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.950
maybe that's the colours he's used. But to

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.500
take this a step further - and this I

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.010
think is one of our favourites. I

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:43.290
think this is one of Brady's favourites

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.160
isn't it? I think we should just

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.110
appreciate the beauty of that for a

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:51.269
moment, before we describe it, before we

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:53.790
ruin really the beauty of it by

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:56.569
describing it. We should just
appreciate that.

00:02:56.569 --> 00:02:59.940
Now what he's done there, he's connected

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.850
the digits together as you go through

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.370
the digits of pi. So he started at 3, and

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.350
then he's connected 3 to 1,

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.350
so you'll go here. And he's connected 1 to

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.019
4, so it will go down here. And he's

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:18.239
connected 4 to 1 so it goes back to 1,

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:22.230
then it'll go to 5, and that'll go to 9,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:25.049
and he's connecting them in that way. And

00:03:25.049 --> 00:03:27.360
he's also given each digit a colour as

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.090
well. So it's like a path, he's made a

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.000
path. He's put the numbers in a circle: 0,

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.630
1, 2, 3, 4, 5 up to 9
and you've made a path.

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.590
And it makes this beautiful circular

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:42.959
piece of art. Data visualisation's hugely

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:45.450
important, it goes way back. We could

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.600
talk about Florence Nightingale, she had

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.480
to represent her statistics that she had

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.820
in the Crimean War when she was a nurse,

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.670
and the deaths that she was suffering on

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.560
the wards, it was because they weren't

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.540
clean enough, and she would represent

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.310
this data- she was a mathematician

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.519
herself, Florence Nightingale fantastic.

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:07.859
And she had these things called Rose

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:10.530
diagrams, which I 
actually think are hideous,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.859
but they are a similar idea. They're

00:04:13.859 --> 00:04:17.100
representing data in a visual way. I mean,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.410
a more simple example of that is a pie

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.169
graph representing data in a visual way.

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:25.530
And what these guys now - and I think it's

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:27.150
become quite a

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.180
recently - is to make
it beautiful as well.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.540
And that's important because to

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.160
communicate especially to the general

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.680
public you want to be able to look at

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.040
this information, understand it, and enjoy

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.480
looking at it and you'll appreciate it

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.460
better. We can talk about serious

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.190
mathematics, subjects like combinatorics,

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:50.490
which is very visual as well. And you can

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:53.520
then do maths through pictures and

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.260
diagrams and things like this; networks

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.840
and graph theory and things like that. In

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.780
this example that we've got here, there

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.030
is actually something we can look at. So

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.610
we think that pi is pretty random;

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:10.800
Martin actually compared pi here with a

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.110
few randomly generated numbers. So he

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.820
generated- these aren't pi, these are

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.890
randomly generated numbers, he did the

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.890
same sort of artistry. And you can see

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:26.550
the similar sorts of patterns coming

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:30.510
through. Pi has the appearance of a

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:34.230
randomly generated number. Another piece

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:38.100
of art by Martin is the same idea again,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.250
our circular paths, but here he's put

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.980
little dots on the outside just to show

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.960
you where the the lines are coming from

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.040
and where they're going to. So if it's

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.180
3.141 then above the 3 he's shown it

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:55.950
with a little dot that it's going to the

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.280
number 1; and then above the 1 it shows

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.590
that he's come from the number 3. It's

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:02.850
just showing where it's going to and

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:03.660
where it's coming from.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:06.030
So the size of dots means it's- that

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.460
this occurs more often; actually look,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.800
look at this one. There's a big purple

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.700
dot. And this shows that it's actually

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.220
purple, it means 9, and it's above the 9

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.410
here. What it's showing is it's- there's a

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.410
there's a sequence in pi that is just

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:26.040
9999 - it's the Feynman point, which I

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:28.890
know Brady is a big fan of, which is six

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.880
consecutive 9s somewhere around the

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:35.870
760th something digit? I think it's 762

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:39.600
digit in pi. And you can see it - that is

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:40.639
there.

00:06:40.639 --> 00:06:43.560
Those are six consecutive 9s and it

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:47.219
makes a big blob because it's coming

00:06:47.219 --> 00:06:49.560
from 9 repeatedly, repeatedly.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:51.300
Martin has done this for other numbers

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:52.590
as well; he's done it for the golden

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.139
ratio, another famous mathematical number.

00:06:55.139 --> 00:06:57.900
He's done this for e, another famous

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.689
mathematical number. And what he was

00:07:00.689 --> 00:07:01.949
interested in though, when he started

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:05.729
doing that, was where they coincide. What

00:07:05.729 --> 00:07:08.389
he did is he lined up these three

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:10.529
special mathematical numbers, he lined

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:13.110
them up and he wanted to look at where

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.199
they had the same digits. Now because

00:07:16.199 --> 00:07:18.889
these numbers are kind of random really,

00:07:18.889 --> 00:07:21.000
when they have the same digit that's

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.449
just random. So for all three numbers to

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:26.340
have the same digit, that happens with

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:29.400
a probability of one in a hundred. But

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.830
when he lined them up he got something

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:34.349
he called the Accidental Similarity

00:07:34.349 --> 00:07:37.439
Number. And he discovered that, yes, they

00:07:37.439 --> 00:07:40.919
do line up with the same digit about one

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:42.779
in a hundred - which is what probability

00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:44.580
tells us - so they have a sort of an

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:47.629
average gap between lining up of about

00:07:47.629 --> 00:07:51.509
100. And he started to experiment with

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.300
his accidental similarity number, he made

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.909
some pieces of art based on that as well.

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:58.860
There you go here's another piece, here's

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.589
another one of our circular ones here.

00:08:01.589 --> 00:08:04.589
And doesn't it look pretty? You should go

00:08:04.589 --> 00:08:06.330
to his website as well where he'll

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:09.750
explain more details about how all these

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.700
things were made.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.940
[Preview] Can you do that again 
and this time talk

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:21.510
me through it as you do it?
- You ask a lot Brady.

